export const laterEssays = {
  'civil-rights-act': essay([
    s('overcoming-the-filibuster', 'Getting a bill through the Senate', p('The Civil Rights Act faced a procedural barrier as well as disagreement over its substance. Opponents used extended Senate debate to prevent a final vote. The measure occupied the chamber for sixty working days. Democratic floor manager Hubert Humphrey and Republican Thomas Kuchel worked to assemble the supermajority then required to close debate; Republican leader Everett Dirksen helped secure the necessary votes. The June 10 cloture vote therefore mattered separately from passage: it broke the obstruction that had repeatedly defeated earlier civil rights proposals.', 'cra-cloture')),
    s('rights-in-daily-life', 'Connecting public places, schools, and work', p('Signed by Lyndon Johnson on July 2, 1964, the statute addressed several settings in which segregation constrained daily life. Its provisions covered public accommodations, public facilities, education, federally assisted programs, and employment. These were related but distinct legal interventions. A restaurant refusing service, a segregated school, and an employer rejecting an applicant did not present identical enforcement problems. Dividing the act into titles gave federal institutions different tools for confronting exclusion rather than relying on a single broad declaration of equality.', 'civil-rights-act')),
    s('employment-machinery', 'Building an agency for employment complaints', p('Title VII prohibited employment discrimination on grounds including race, color, religion, sex, and national origin and created the Equal Employment Opportunity Commission. The commission began operating on July 2, 1965, with roughly one hundred employees. That one-year interval separates enactment from the opening of the agency intended to administer the employment provisions. Employers, unions, and employment agencies were part of this framework. The new institution made workplace discrimination a continuing subject of federal administration, complaint handling, and public accountability.', 'eeoc-timeline')),
    s('enforcement-developed', 'A foundation that required further action', p('The 1964 act changed federal law, but its machinery continued to develop. Initially the EEOC could not sue employers directly; it later participated in litigation through briefs supporting the courts’ consideration of employment disputes. Congress gave it authority to sue private employers in 1972. This sequence helps explain why the signing ceremony should not be read as the instant completion of workplace equality. The statute created enforceable commitments, while later legislation altered the capacity of the agency responsible for making those commitments effective.', 'eeoc-timeline')),
  ], [t('June 10, 1964', 'The Senate invokes cloture on the civil rights bill.', 'cra-cloture'), t('July 2, 1964', 'Johnson signs the Civil Rights Act.', 'civil-rights-act'), t('July 2, 1965', 'The EEOC begins operating.', 'eeoc-timeline')], p('The enrolled act establishes what Congress enacted; the Senate account explains the procedural struggle. The EEOC chronology distinguishes its creation from later enforcement powers. These institutional records document legal changes rather than measuring every community’s experience of them.', 'civil-rights-act', 'cra-cloture', 'eeoc-timeline')),

  'selma-marches': essay([
    s('organizing-before-the-bridge', 'A campaign already rooted in Selma', p('The bridge confrontation grew out of sustained local work. The Dallas County Voters League, SNCC organizers, and Black residents had pressed for registration before Martin Luther King Jr. and SCLC joined the campaign in January 1965. Repeated attempts to register had left only a small share of local Black residents on the rolls. In nearby Marion, a state trooper shot Jimmie Lee Jackson during the suppression of a February demonstration. His death helped turn organizers toward a march carrying their demands to the Alabama state capital.', 'selma-stanford')),
    s('churches-as-infrastructure', 'Where marchers gathered', p('Brown Chapel AME Church supplied more than a recognizable starting point. It hosted SCLC during the opening months of 1965, while nearby First Baptist Church housed SNCC’s organizing headquarters. These buildings offered the campaign places to assemble and coordinate. On March 7, about six hundred protesters gathered at Brown Chapel despite Governor George Wallace’s prohibition on the march. Hosea Williams and John Lewis led them toward Montgomery. The church’s role connects the nationally remembered confrontation to the local institutions that helped make collective action possible.', 'selma')),
    s('attack-and-return', 'Bloody Sunday and a second attempt', p('At the Edmund Pettus Bridge, state troopers and local officers ordered the marchers to disperse, then attacked them with clubs and tear gas. Lewis suffered a fractured skull. Television coverage carried the violence to viewers far beyond Alabama, and additional supporters came to Selma. King led a second procession to the bridge on March 9 and turned it back after prayer. That return was a distinct action, not the completed march to Montgomery; it took place while organizers were still negotiating the constraints of federal court proceedings.', 'selma', 'selma-stanford')),
    s('march-and-legislation', 'Reaching Montgomery', p('The protected march began on March 21 and reached Montgomery on March 25. Participants traveled fifty-four miles, with numbers restricted along a narrow highway segment and swelling for the final rally. The campaign made the distance between constitutional voting rights and actual access visible to a national audience. Its legislative consequence was not a law signed on the courthouse steps: Congress continued its work, and Johnson signed the Voting Rights Act in August. The marches and the statute were connected stages involving local organizers, demonstrators, judges, and federal lawmakers.', 'selma-stanford', 'voting-rights-act')),
  ], [t('March 7, 1965', 'Officers attack the first march at the Edmund Pettus Bridge.', 'selma'), t('March 9, 1965', 'King leads a procession to the bridge and back.', 'selma'), t('March 21–25, 1965', 'The federally protected march travels to Montgomery.', 'selma-stanford')], p('The National Park Service places churches and the route at the center of its account. Stanford’s encyclopedia supplies the campaign chronology and identifies local organizations. The two perspectives help keep the story from becoming only a biography of its most famous leader.', 'selma', 'selma-stanford')),

  'voting-rights-act': essay([
    s('why-another-law', 'Why existing promises were insufficient', p('The Voting Rights Act responded to a recurring enforcement failure. Constitutional prohibitions and earlier federal lawsuits had not stopped officials from replacing one discriminatory registration practice with another. The Justice Department’s historical account explains how litigation could restart each time a barrier changed. A person might win a ruling while the local registration system continued to exclude other applicants. Congress therefore considered more than whether racial discrimination was unlawful; it confronted how to prevent repeated obstruction from exhausting the people and institutions trying to challenge it.', 'voting-law-history')),
    s('different-sections', 'Several tools with different jobs', p('The act signed on August 6, 1965 combined a nationwide prohibition with targeted measures. Section 2 prohibited racial discrimination in voting across the country. In jurisdictions identified under the coverage provisions, literacy tests were suspended and federal examiners could register eligible applicants. Section 5 required approval before covered jurisdictions implemented changes in voting practices. These functions should not be collapsed together: registering a voter, prohibiting a discriminatory rule, and reviewing a proposed change addressed different points at which political participation could be obstructed.', 'voting-rights-act')),
    s('registration-and-political-power', 'From registration to the use of a ballot', p('Federal enforcement brought substantial registration gains, but registering more voters did not settle every contest over political power. The Justice Department records that later congressional hearings examined district boundaries, annexations, and at-large election systems that could weaken newly registered voters’ influence. Congress extended the special provisions in 1970 and 1975; the latter amendments also addressed discrimination against language minorities. The continuing revisions show lawmakers responding to the structure of elections as well as the individual act of entering a name on a voter roll.', 'voting-law-history')),
    s('changing-enforcement', 'A law with a subsequent history', p('Later judicial decisions altered how parts of the law operated. In 2013, Shelby County v. Holder invalidated the Section 4(b) formula used to determine automatic coverage under Section 5. The Court did not invalidate the entire act or Section 5 itself. The Justice Department explains that jurisdictions covered only by that formula no longer had to submit changes for preclearance, while a separate court order could still impose coverage. Understanding the 1965 achievement therefore requires separating the original law from changes to its enforcement decades afterward.', 'shelby')),
  ], [t('August 6, 1965', 'Johnson signs the Voting Rights Act.', 'voting-rights-act'), t('1975', 'Congress extends the special provisions and adds language-minority protections.', 'voting-law-history'), t('June 25, 2013', 'Shelby County invalidates the automatic coverage formula.', 'shelby')], p('The original statute is evidence of the 1965 legal framework. The Justice Department histories describe later implementation and amendments from the enforcing agency’s perspective. This essay follows those historical changes and does not present the original text as a complete account of present election law.', 'voting-rights-act', 'voting-law-history', 'shelby')),

  'black-panther-party': essay([
    s('oakland-origins', 'An organization formed in Oakland', p('Huey P. Newton and Bobby Seale founded the Black Panther Party for Self-Defense in Oakland in October 1966 after meeting at Merritt College. The party arose within the Black Power movement and combined Black nationalism, socialist ideas, and armed self-defense. Police violence was central to its organizing, but its program also addressed economic and political control. Its name drew on the panther symbol used by Alabama’s Lowndes County Freedom Organization, linking a new West Coast organization to another experiment in independent Black political action.', 'panthers-archives')),
    s('ten-point-demands', 'A program larger than a single tactic', p('The party’s Ten-Point Program connected demands for self-determination to employment, housing, education, and the treatment of Black people by police and courts. Reading the program as a political document reveals how its authors understood these problems as connected. It articulated what the organization wanted to change, rather than proving that all of those goals had been achieved. The breadth of the demands also helps explain why an account limited to weapons or confrontations misses the economic grievances and community aspirations the party publicly advanced.', 'panthers-program')),
    s('community-programs', 'Food and care as organizing work', p('The Panthers developed practical programs alongside their confrontational public politics. Their activities included food assistance, health screening, legal aid, and adult education. Oakland’s Free Breakfast for School Children program began in 1969. Such programs made the party’s presence tangible in daily life: children could receive food, and residents could seek services where resources were scarce. The breakfast program also attracted national attention. Community provision was part of political organizing, allowing the party to argue that collective action could meet immediate needs while challenging the conditions producing those needs.', 'panthers-archives', 'panthers-breakfast')),
    s('reading-a-contested-record', 'Surveillance and the historical record', p('The party was targeted by the FBI’s COINTELPRO operation and by state and local law enforcement. The National Archives preserves records produced by those agencies alongside films, recordings, and other material about the organization. These sources require attention to who created them and for what purpose. An investigative file records what an agency gathered or alleged; it is not automatically a neutral account of every member’s actions. Reading government records beside the party’s own program gives a fuller view of its political aims and the state response.', 'panthers-archives', 'panthers-program')),
  ], [t('October 1966', 'Newton and Seale establish the party in Oakland.', 'panthers-archives'), t('1966', 'The Ten-Point Program sets out the organization’s demands.', 'panthers-program'), t('1969', 'The Oakland breakfast program begins.', 'panthers-breakfast')], p('The party’s program is evidence of its stated goals, while the National Archives guide describes government holdings and their origins. The Oakland library history addresses a local service program. These perspectives should be read together; neither a manifesto nor a surveillance archive alone represents every participant’s experience.', 'panthers-program', 'panthers-archives', 'panthers-breakfast')),

  'thurgood-marshall': essay([
    s('lawyer-before-justice', 'Civil rights work before appointment', p('Thurgood Marshall’s path to the Supreme Court began in legal training and collective litigation. At Howard University Law School, Charles Hamilton Houston mentored him in the use of law to challenge racial inequality. Marshall later worked with Black residents seeking to contest segregated schooling in Clarendon County, South Carolina. Their case became part of the litigation associated with Brown v. Board of Education. This background matters because his appointment brought to the bench experience developed through clients, local communities, and sustained challenges to institutions that enforced segregation.', 'marshall-nps')),
    s('a-record-of-public-service', 'From advocate to federal official', p('The chronology in Marshall’s papers records a career across different legal roles: NAACP litigation, service on a federal appeals court, and work as solicitor general before the Supreme Court appointment. These posts were not interchangeable. As an advocate he argued for clients; as solicitor general he represented the federal government before the Court; as a judge he participated in deciding cases. His accumulated experience helps explain why the 1967 nomination represented both a historic change in representation and the elevation of an established national legal figure.', 'marshall-papers')),
    s('confirmation-and-ceremony', 'The Senate vote and the public oath', p('President Lyndon Johnson nominated Marshall on June 13, 1967. The Senate confirmed him on August 30 by a vote of 69 to 11. Confirmation authorized the appointment but was distinct from the public ceremony held on October 2, when Chief Justice Earl Warren administered the oath before Marshall’s family. Keeping these dates separate prevents the timeline from treating the vote and ceremonial entry as one event. Marshall became the first African American justice on a court whose decisions had long shaped the boundaries of Black citizenship.', 'marshall', 'court-nominations')),
    s('service-and-preservation', 'A judicial career beyond the first', p('Marshall remained on the Court until retiring in 1991. The Library of Congress account emphasizes his opinions defending civil rights and his opposition to the death penalty, including dissents when his views did not command a majority. That record makes the significance of his service larger than an appointment-day milestone. His papers, donated to the Library of Congress, preserve correspondence, case materials, and other working records. They allow researchers to investigate how a justice worked within the Court rather than relying only on ceremonial portraits and public tributes.', 'marshall', 'marshall-papers')),
  ], [t('June 13, 1967', 'Johnson submits Marshall’s nomination.', 'court-nominations'), t('August 30, 1967', 'The Senate confirms Marshall, 69–11.', 'court-nominations'), t('October 2, 1967', 'Marshall participates in his public swearing-in.', 'marshall'), t('1991', 'Marshall retires from the Court.', 'marshall')], p('The Senate supplies the nomination and vote record. The Library of Congress anniversary account describes the ceremony, while its finding aid identifies material available for deeper study. The NPS biography supplies litigation context; ceremonial dates alone cannot summarize Marshall’s jurisprudence.', 'court-nominations', 'marshall', 'marshall-papers', 'marshall-nps')),

  'king-assassination': essay([
    s('workers-and-danger', 'The labor struggle that brought King to Memphis', p('Memphis sanitation workers were already confronting dangerous conditions before King arrived. On February 1, 1968, two Black workers were crushed when a garbage truck’s compactor activated. More than eleven hundred workers began a strike on February 12, seeking safety, improved pay and benefits, and recognition of their union. The National Archives preserves documents from the conflict, including the city’s attempt to restrict demonstrations. These records place King’s final campaign within a labor dispute initiated by workers whose demands reached beyond the right to use desegregated public facilities.', 'memphis-documents')),
    s('a-campaign-under-pressure', 'Nonviolent protest and economic dignity', p('King supported the strike as part of a struggle linking racial equality with economic security. His involvement did not replace the local campaign: sanitation workers, union leaders, and ministers had organized its meetings and demands. A March demonstration ended in violence, and King returned to help lead another march. On April 3 he addressed supporters at Mason Temple. The setting makes his final public activity concrete: he was assisting workers seeking an agreement from their employer while trying to sustain nonviolent collective protest under severe pressure.', 'memphis-strike')),
    s('april-four', 'The killing at the Lorraine Motel', p('On April 4, King was shot while standing on a balcony of the Lorraine Motel. He was taken to St. Joseph’s Hospital and pronounced dead that evening. His death prompted national mourning and upheaval in many cities. The King Institute’s account documents the immediate sequence and the response of his colleagues and family. Remembering the assassination in its Memphis setting connects the loss of a national leader to a specific unfinished campaign, rather than allowing the violence of his death to displace the work that had brought him there.', 'king')),
    s('the-strike-continued', 'Mourning and unfinished organizing', p('Coretta Scott King joined a Memphis march on April 8 honoring her husband and supporting the sanitation workers. His funeral followed in Atlanta on April 9. The strike did not end with those commemorations: an agreement was reached on April 16. That sequence preserves the workers as historical actors who continued to pursue their demands after King’s death. It also distinguishes public grief from the practical negotiations required to resolve a labor conflict. The campaign’s outcome depended on ongoing organizing, even as its most prominent supporter was mourned.', 'king', 'memphis-strike')),
  ], [t('February 12, 1968', 'Sanitation workers begin their strike.', 'memphis-documents'), t('April 4, 1968', 'King is assassinated at the Lorraine Motel.', 'king'), t('April 8, 1968', 'Coretta Scott King joins the workers’ Memphis march.', 'king'), t('April 16, 1968', 'The strike ends with an agreement.', 'memphis-strike')], p('The King Institute provides the assassination and strike chronologies. The National Archives educational collection includes legal records generated during the conflict; those records reflect the positions of parties to a dispute. Reading them together keeps labor organizing visible alongside national mourning.', 'king', 'memphis-strike', 'memphis-documents')),
  'fair-housing-act': essay([
    s('a-separate-legislative-fight', 'Housing remained a legislative battleground', p('Federal civil rights legislation did not arrive as a single finished package. Housing required its own prolonged legislative struggle after the 1964 act. The Justice Department’s history traces proposals to prohibit discrimination in sales, rentals, and financing and disagreements about how enforcement should work. Johnson renewed the demand in 1967. These debates concerned where families could live and whether private transactions would be subject to federal intervention. The eventual statute emerged from negotiation over both the scope of the prohibition and the institutions charged with carrying it out.', 'housing-enforcement')),
    s('april-1968', 'Passage in the aftermath of King’s death', p('The Senate passed the Civil Rights Act of 1968, including its housing title, on March 11. The measure was still before the House when Martin Luther King Jr. was assassinated on April 4. Johnson urged the House to act; it approved the Senate bill on April 10, and he signed it the following day. That chronology shows how the assassination intensified the pressure surrounding an existing legislative effort. Fair housing had advocates and proposals before the national crisis; the law was neither conceived nor completed in a single day.', 'housing-enforcement')),
    s('what-the-law-addressed', 'Transactions and federal responsibility', p('Title VIII, known as the Fair Housing Act, initially prohibited discrimination based on race, color, religion, and national origin. Its housing protections concerned renting, purchasing, financing, and related activities. The statute also directed federal departments and agencies to administer housing and urban development programs in ways that furthered fair housing. That second responsibility matters: the law addressed government action as well as conduct within individual transactions. The original categories should be stated accurately, without treating later amendments as though they had all been present in 1968.', 'fair-housing')),
    s('later-protections', 'An expanding enforcement framework', p('The act’s protections and enforcement powers developed after enactment. Sex was added as a protected ground later, and Congress expanded the law in 1988 to cover disability and familial status while strengthening HUD’s enforcement authority. These amendments illustrate why a historical account must distinguish the founding statute from subsequent versions. A federal prohibition also did not itself document the disappearance of discrimination in the housing market. HUD’s continuing enforcement work and the amendments belong to the history of implementation, separate from the fact that Congress established the protections.', 'fair-housing', 'housing-1988')),
  ], [t('March 11, 1968', 'The Senate passes legislation containing the housing title.', 'housing-enforcement'), t('April 11, 1968', 'Johnson signs the Fair Housing Act as Title VIII.', 'fair-housing'), t('1988', 'Congress expands protected grounds and enforcement powers.', 'housing-1988')], p('HUD’s anniversary account identifies the original protected grounds and later expansion. The Justice Department narrative reconstructs the legislative negotiations from an enforcement institution’s perspective. The essay describes historical enactments and does not substitute the 1968 framework for current legal guidance.', 'fair-housing', 'housing-enforcement', 'housing-1988')),

  'shirley-chisholm': essay([
    s('brooklyn-to-congress', 'A local political career becomes a national first', p('Shirley Chisholm entered congressional politics with experience as an educator, community activist, and New York state legislator. In November 1968, she won a newly created Brooklyn district, becoming the first Black woman elected to Congress. Her House service began with the 91st Congress on January 3, 1969. The election and the beginning of her term were separate milestones. The House historian also records that she was the only woman in that freshman class, placing her achievement within the institution’s sharply limited female representation.', 'chisholm')),
    s('committee-work', 'Making representation useful to constituents', p('Chisholm’s first assignment to the Agriculture Committee posed a practical question about how a representative from an urban district could use congressional power. She obtained different assignments and worked on food assistance, including expansion of food stamps and the development of the program known as WIC. These efforts connect her earlier work with children and families to federal policymaking. Her legislative concerns also included racial and gender equality and opposition to the Vietnam War. The record of her service therefore extends well beyond the symbolic importance of being first.', 'chisholm-archives')),
    s('education-and-care', 'A legislative agenda centered on daily needs', p('The House history emphasizes Chisholm’s support for education funding, longer operating hours for daycare facilities, and income support for families. It also identifies her work on national school lunch legislation and the effort to override President Gerald Ford’s veto. Such measures show what representation meant in budgetary and legislative terms: deciding whether institutions that supported children and working families would receive federal resources. Chisholm used her seat to place these needs before colleagues rather than treating arrival in Congress as the completion of her political work.', 'chisholm')),
    s('presidential-campaign-and-service', 'Organizing beyond one district', p('In 1971, Chisholm became a founding member of the Congressional Black Caucus. The next year she sought the Democratic presidential nomination, bringing her candidacy to a national audience without winning the nomination. She continued to serve in the House after the campaign and held seven terms altogether, leaving Congress in 1983. That continuity matters: the presidential bid was part of an extended political career, not a replacement for legislative service. Her work crossed local representation, congressional coalition building, and a challenge to expectations about who could seek national executive power.', 'chisholm-archives')),
  ], [t('November 1968', 'Chisholm wins election to the House.', 'chisholm'), t('January 3, 1969', 'Her congressional service begins.', 'chisholm'), t('1972', 'She seeks the Democratic presidential nomination.', 'chisholm-archives'), t('1983', 'She leaves Congress after seven terms.', 'chisholm-archives')], p('House and National Archives histories identify the dates of election and service and document policy priorities. They are institutional summaries rather than a full account of district politics or every bill. The election milestone is kept separate from the later start of her congressional term.', 'chisholm', 'chisholm-archives')),

  'black-history-month': essay([
    s('building-the-field', 'The scholarship behind the observance', p('Black History Month rests on institutions built before a president recognized it. Carter G. Woodson co-founded the Association for the Study of Negro Life and History in 1915 to address the exclusion of Black people from public understanding of the American past. His work included research, teaching, and training other scholars. He established the Journal of Negro History in 1916 and a publishing operation in 1921. These projects created ways to produce and circulate historical knowledge, making commemoration part of a larger intellectual and educational enterprise.', 'woodson-nps')),
    s('from-week-to-month', 'A calendar tied to public memory', p('Woodson introduced Negro History Week in 1926. February linked the observance to the birthdays of Abraham Lincoln and Frederick Douglass, already familiar figures in emancipation memory. Over the following decades, educators and activists brought Black history into classrooms and public programs. The Ford Library’s account identifies college campuses and the civil rights era as part of the observance’s growing reach. The move toward a month therefore built on an established practice of collective study and commemoration, rather than introducing Black historical scholarship for the first time.', 'ford-history-month')),
    s('recognition-in-1976', 'The meaning of presidential recognition', p('In February 1976, during the nation’s Bicentennial year, President Gerald Ford issued a message recognizing Black History Month. ASALH had urged presidential recognition; Ford chose a message rather than the requested proclamation. The distinction is useful because the episode is sometimes compressed into a claim that a president created the observance. His action gave national executive recognition to work already carried by historians, teachers, and communities. The Bicentennial setting also raised a larger question: whose experiences would be included when the country told its two-hundred-year story?', 'ford-history-month')),
    s('study-beyond-the-calendar', 'An educational network rather than a single ceremony', p('Woodson’s organization worked with churches, schools, colleges, libraries, and community centers. Teachers sought materials and guidance, and the organization helped develop researchers who trained later generations of historians. This institutional history explains why the observance cannot be reduced to a list of famous firsts. Its foundations included access to books, publication opportunities, and the ability to teach a history omitted from conventional curricula. The month offers a recurring moment for that work; the research and educational relationships that made it possible extended throughout the year.', 'woodson-nps')),
  ], [t('1915', 'Woodson co-founds the historical association.', 'woodson-nps'), t('1926', 'Negro History Week begins.', 'ford-history-month'), t('February 10, 1976', 'Ford issues his message recognizing Black History Month.', 'ford-history-month')], p('The NPS biography explains Woodson’s institution building. The Ford presidential archive distinguishes the 1976 message from earlier community work and later official recognition. These sources document a developing observance, rather than a single law that created all Black history teaching.', 'woodson-nps', 'ford-history-month')),

  'mae-jemison': essay([
    s('medicine-and-preparation', 'A scientific career before spaceflight', p('Mae Jemison trained in engineering and medicine before becoming an astronaut. After medical school, she worked as a physician and served as a Peace Corps medical officer in Sierra Leone and Liberia from 1983 to 1985. NASA selected her as an astronaut candidate in 1987. Her medical background is important to the story of the flight: the astronaut was already a professional with experience connecting scientific knowledge to practical care. The National Library of Medicine places spaceflight within that wider career rather than treating it as an isolated achievement.', 'jemison-medicine')),
    s('launch-day-work', 'September 12 at Kennedy Space Center', p('Jemison launched aboard Endeavour on September 12, 1992, becoming the first Black woman in space. NASA’s account of launch preparations identifies suit technician Sharon McDougle checking Jemison’s pressure suit at Kennedy Space Center. That detail makes visible some of the specialized work supporting the milestone. An astronaut’s flight depended on a team as well as individual preparation. Jemison served as science mission specialist on STS-47, an eight-day mission carried out with Japan’s space agency, placing her achievement inside a specific international research program.', 'jemison')),
    s('spacelab-research', 'The mission’s scientific purpose', p('STS-47 carried the Spacelab-J research program, with twenty-four materials-science experiments and twenty life-science experiments. NASA’s mission record describes a crew divided into teams for around-the-clock operations. The work examined how processes and living systems behaved in the spaceflight environment. This research purpose matters when interpreting the historic first: Jemison was a working member of a crew responsible for experiments and equipment, not simply a passenger representing a demographic milestone. The mission connected the symbolic opening of astronaut opportunities with the practical tasks of scientific investigation.', 'sts47-mission')),
    s('return-and-broader-career', 'A flight within a longer public career', p('Endeavour returned on September 20, completing the mission. Jemison’s wider career included advocacy for young people’s participation in science and medicine and for greater diversity within those fields. Her recollections in the National Library of Medicine exhibition discuss early curiosity and expectations about what girls could become. Those recollections provide a different kind of evidence from a flight log: they explain how she remembered her path toward science. Together, career history and mission records show both the preparation behind the flight and the educational significance attached to it afterward.', 'sts47-mission', 'jemison-medicine')),
  ], [t('1987', 'NASA selects Jemison for astronaut training.', 'jemison-medicine'), t('September 12, 1992', 'STS-47 launches with Jemison aboard.', 'jemison'), t('September 20, 1992', 'Endeavour returns from STS-47.', 'sts47-mission')], p('NASA records establish the mission dates, crew work, and experiments. The National Library of Medicine biography and recollections add medical training and personal context. A retrospective portrait and a mission record answer different questions; neither alone explains every barrier to participation in spaceflight.', 'jemison', 'sts47-mission', 'jemison-medicine')),

  'obama-election': essay([
    s('an-illinois-political-career', 'The career preceding the campaign', p('Barack Obama’s national candidacy followed work in community organizing, law, and Illinois politics. He moved to Chicago in 1985 to work with the Developing Communities Project, later attended Harvard Law School, and returned to legal and civic work in the city. Elected to the Illinois Senate in 1996, he subsequently won a U.S. Senate seat in 2004. This sequence places the 2008 milestone within a political career built through several institutions, rather than presenting the presidential election as a sudden entrance into public life.', 'obama')),
    s('nomination-and-election', 'Becoming the Democratic nominee', p('Obama announced his presidential candidacy on February 10, 2007 and accepted the Democratic nomination on August 28, 2008. He won the November 4 election, becoming the first African American elected president. His running mate was Delaware senator Joe Biden. The presidential library’s chronology distinguishes the announcement, party nomination, election, and inauguration. Those stages represent different decisions by a candidate, a party, and voters; keeping them separate helps explain the process that culminated in a historic change in who would hold the nation’s highest office.', 'obama', 'election-2008')),
    s('the-electoral-record', 'What the official result shows', p('The National Archives records 365 electoral votes for Obama and 173 for Republican nominee John McCain, with a majority requiring 270. Biden and Republican vice-presidential nominee Sarah Palin received the corresponding totals. The state table documents how those votes accumulated across jurisdictions. Nebraska split its electoral votes between the tickets, awarding Obama one and McCain four under its district-based system. This detail is a reminder that the constitutional election result was assembled through state electoral processes, even as the outcome was understood and celebrated as a national milestone.', 'election-2008')),
    s('election-and-office', 'The transition to the presidency', p('Obama’s victory did not make him president on election night. He resigned his Senate seat in November 2008 and was inaugurated as the forty-fourth president on January 20, 2009. The distinction matters for a historical timeline because an election result and the exercise of executive office occur at different moments. His election established a first in presidential representation. Assessing particular policies or their effects requires separate evidence from the administration’s record; the electoral result itself establishes who won and the date of the political breakthrough.', 'obama', 'election-2008')),
  ], [t('February 10, 2007', 'Obama announces his presidential candidacy.', 'obama'), t('November 4, 2008', 'Obama wins election as president.', 'obama'), t('January 20, 2009', 'Obama is inaugurated.', 'obama')], p('The presidential library provides a career chronology, while the National Archives electoral table records the official outcome. These establish the milestone and its timing; neither a biography nor electoral totals should be treated as a comprehensive assessment of the presidency that followed.', 'obama', 'election-2008')),

  'shelby-county': essay([
    s('a-preventive-system', 'What preclearance was designed to do', p('The Voting Rights Act’s preclearance system required certain jurisdictions to obtain federal approval before implementing changes to voting practices. It responded to a history in which officials could substitute new barriers after older ones were struck down. The coverage formula determined where this extra requirement applied. Congress repeatedly renewed the special provisions, most recently before this case in 2006. Shelby County, Alabama, challenged that arrangement, bringing to the Supreme Court a dispute about whether the geographic basis for the special oversight remained constitutionally justified.', 'voting-law-history', 'shelby-opinion')),
    s('the-majority', 'The majority’s objection to the formula', p('On June 25, 2013, a five-justice majority invalidated Section 4(b). Chief Justice John Roberts’s opinion argued that the formula continued to impose different obligations on states using old measures of voting tests and participation. The majority acknowledged the Voting Rights Act’s success and the persistence of voting discrimination, but concluded that the formula was not adequately connected to contemporary conditions. This was the Court’s constitutional reasoning, not a finding that racial discrimination had disappeared or that every voting procedure in a previously covered jurisdiction was fair.', 'shelby-opinion')),
    s('the-dissent', 'A different reading of the same history', p('Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg dissented, joined by Justices Stephen Breyer, Sonia Sotomayor, and Elena Kagan. The dissent treated improvements under the law as evidence of effective protection and emphasized Congress’s record concerning continuing discrimination. The disagreement therefore involved how to interpret success: whether it weakened the justification for exceptional oversight or demonstrated why that oversight remained useful. Reading the opposing opinions makes clear that the case was a contested judgment about constitutional authority and evidence, rather than an uncontested historical conclusion about the end of disenfranchisement.', 'shelby-opinion')),
    s('what-changed', 'The decision’s immediate legal consequence', p('The ruling did not invalidate Section 5 itself or the entire Voting Rights Act. The Justice Department explains its operational consequence: jurisdictions covered only by the Section 4(b) formula no longer needed preclearance for new voting changes. Separate coverage imposed by court order under Section 3(c) remained possible. Its list of previously covered jurisdictions also shows that coverage had included whole states and individual counties, not simply one undifferentiated region. These distinctions are essential to understanding the historical change in federal oversight without overstating the scope of the decision.', 'shelby')),
  ], [t('2006', 'Congress renews the special voting-rights provisions.', 'voting-law-history'), t('February 27, 2013', 'The Supreme Court hears argument in Shelby County.', 'shelby-opinion'), t('June 25, 2013', 'The Court invalidates the Section 4(b) coverage formula.', 'shelby')], p('The judicial text contains the majority, concurrence, and dissent, which must be identified as different positions. The Justice Department page describes the ruling’s effect on preclearance. This account explains the 2013 decision rather than offering an up-to-date guide to every later voting-rights dispute.', 'shelby-opinion', 'shelby')),

  'black-lives-matter': essay([
    s('the-2013-response', 'A name emerges from grief and organizing', p('Black Lives Matter emerged in 2013 after George Zimmerman was acquitted in the killing of Trayvon Martin. Alicia Garza, Patrisse Cullors, and Opal Tometi developed the project. The National Women’s History Museum places Cullors’s response within an existing career of organizing against policing and incarceration practices. The origins therefore involved experienced activists as well as digital communication. The phrase gave a shared public form to grief and protest, but the people who developed it brought earlier relationships, political commitments, and organizing experience to the work.', 'cullors-museum', 'blm-origin')),
    s('women-and-organizing', 'Leadership that shaped the project', p('Cullors’s biography connects her activism to experiences of her family and community with policing and imprisonment. It also situates her work in organizations including Dignity and Power Now and in advocacy concerning gender, race, and the treatment of incarcerated people. This context helps explain why the movement’s beginnings should include its Black women organizers rather than only the deaths that brought public attention. The founders were making choices about whose experiences to center and how to connect struggles over state violence with wider demands for dignity and political power.', 'cullors-museum')),
    s('ferguson-and-local-networks', 'From a shared phrase to organized connections', p('The organization’s retrospective identifies the 2014 protests after Michael Brown’s killing in Ferguson as a turning point. Patrisse Cullors and Darnell Moore organized a Labor Day weekend ride bringing supporters to the St. Louis area. Its account describes participants returning home to build local chapters and credits the sustained work of Ferguson organizers. This is evidence of how the network understood its own growth: national visibility depended on local action, travel, relationships, and continuing commitments, not simply on the circulation of a hashtag.', 'blm-origin')),
    s('movement-and-organization', 'Reading the name carefully', p('The network’s origin account describes a decentralized structure intended to develop local leadership and challenge violence against Black communities. That organizational history is one part of the larger public use of Black Lives Matter as a movement name and rallying phrase. A specific foundation’s account should not be treated as proof that it directed every protest carried out under the name. Read with the museum biography, it supports a history of founding organizers and network building while leaving room for the varied people and local campaigns associated with the broader movement.', 'blm-origin', 'cullors-museum')),
  ], [t('2013', 'Garza, Cullors, and Tometi develop the Black Lives Matter project.', 'blm-origin'), t('2014', 'Ferguson becomes a major focus of organizing after Michael Brown’s killing.', 'blm-origin'), t('Labor Day weekend, 2014', 'Organizers bring supporters to the St. Louis area for the Black Life Matters Ride.', 'blm-origin')], p('The foundation’s origin story is a participant account of its own network, written retrospectively in 2018. The museum biography provides context about Cullors. Organizational claims are attributed to that account; they do not establish a single leadership structure for the entire movement.', 'blm-origin', 'cullors-museum')),

  'nmaahc-opens': essay([
    s('institution-before-building', 'The museum existed before its doors opened', p('The National Museum of African American History and Culture was established in 2003, thirteen years before its National Mall building opened to the public. The Smithsonian’s institutional chronology distinguishes the creation of the museum from the completion of a permanent home near the Washington Monument. That interval matters because a museum is more than a building: its work requires assembling collections, developing exhibitions, and creating ways for the public to encounter historical evidence. The 2016 opening marked the arrival of that work at a dedicated national site.', 'smithsonian-museums')),
    s('history-community-culture', 'A broad account of African American life', p('The museum’s inaugural exhibition plan grouped its displays around history, community, and culture. Its historical galleries addressed slavery and freedom and the era of segregation, while other exhibitions explored cultural expression and community life. This structure refused to confine African American history to either suffering or celebrated achievement. Objects and interpreted stories could connect political struggle with work, belief, family, and creativity. The exhibition announcement documents the curatorial framework presented to the first visitors, rather than implying that one museum could contain every Black experience.', 'museum-exhibitions')),
    s('opening-day', 'A public ceremony on the National Mall', p('The museum opened on September 24, 2016. President Barack Obama participated in the opening, and a three-day festival surrounded the occasion. The Smithsonian Archives account describes staff from across the institution helping with programming, tickets, directions, and visitor assistance. Those details reveal the practical work behind a nationally visible celebration. The opening was both a ceremonial recognition of African American history and the beginning of daily operation, requiring the museum to welcome visitors and help them navigate a substantial new public institution.', 'museum-anniversary')),
    s('an-ongoing-collection', 'The work continued after the celebration', p('In its first-anniversary retrospective, the Smithsonian reported nearly three million visits and growth of the collections by about three thousand objects. These figures belong to that initial period, not to the museum’s present totals. They show that the opening was a beginning rather than a completed archive. Collecting and interpretation continued as people encountered the galleries and contributed material. The institution’s own documentation of exhibitions, collaboration, and public response also became evidence for later historians studying how African American history received a permanent place on the National Mall.', 'museum-anniversary')),
  ], [t('2003', 'The museum is established.', 'smithsonian-museums'), t('September 24, 2016', 'The National Mall building opens to the public.', 'museum-anniversary'), t('September 2017', 'The Smithsonian reviews the museum’s first year of visits and collecting.', 'museum-anniversary')], p('Smithsonian sources document its own institution, exhibition plans, and opening-year operations. Their perspective is celebratory and curatorial; attendance figures are dated reports, not current statistics. The exhibition plan identifies what the museum intended to present rather than standing in for every visitor’s interpretation.', 'smithsonian-museums', 'museum-exhibitions', 'museum-anniversary')),

  'kamala-harris': essay([
    s('california-and-the-senate', 'A career in elected and legal office', p('Kamala Harris’s route to the vice presidency included legal training and public office in California. She earned degrees from Howard University and the University of California’s Hastings College of the Law, worked in prosecution, and served as San Francisco district attorney and California attorney general. Elected to the U.S. Senate in 2016, she began serving in January 2017. The congressional biography establishes this professional and electoral sequence, placing the 2021 milestone within an existing career rather than reducing it to the image of an inauguration.', 'harris-biography')),
    s('the-2020-election', 'The election preceding the oath', p('Harris was elected vice president on the Democratic ticket headed by Joseph Biden in 2020. The National Archives records 306 electoral votes for the winning ticket and 232 for Donald Trump and Michael Pence. These official results distinguish the vice-presidential election from the later ceremony at which Harris assumed office. They also preserve the state certificates underlying the totals, allowing readers to move from the national result to the documents produced by individual jurisdictions. Election, counting the electoral votes, and inauguration were separate stages.', 'election-2020')),
    s('counting-and-transition', 'A contested transition with a recorded result', p('Congress’s counting of the electoral votes was interrupted by violence at the Capitol on January 6, 2021. The National Archives notes that objections concerning Arizona and Pennsylvania were not sustained and that the result was certified early on January 7. Harris subsequently resigned her Senate seat on January 18 before becoming vice president. These dates clarify the transfer between offices: her Senate service ended before her vice-presidential term began. They also locate the historic inauguration within a disrupted but completed constitutional process for counting the election result.', 'election-2020', 'harris-biography')),
    s('january-twenty', 'Several firsts in one office', p('On January 20, 2021, Harris became vice president and president of the Senate. The Senate’s California chronology identifies her as the first woman, first African American, and first Asian American to serve in those positions. Her South Asian family background is part of the significance recorded in accounts of this milestone. The event changed who had occupied an office long closed to women and to people with those backgrounds. This entry concerns the start of her service in 2021; the historical first is distinct from an evaluation of every policy associated with the administration.', 'harris-california', 'harris-biography')),
  ], [t('January 3, 2017', 'Harris begins serving in the U.S. Senate.', 'harris-biography'), t('January 7, 2021', 'Congress completes certification of the electoral result.', 'election-2020'), t('January 18, 2021', 'Harris resigns her Senate seat.', 'harris-biography'), t('January 20, 2021', 'She takes office as vice president.', 'harris-california')], p('Congressional biographies and the Senate chronology establish offices and dates. The National Archives records the electoral outcome and interruption of its count. These sources substantiate a historical first; they are not a claim about the office’s current holder or a comprehensive account of Harris’s policies.', 'harris-biography', 'harris-california', 'election-2020')),

  'juneteenth-federal-holiday': essay([
    s('the-date-being-remembered', 'June 19 refers to an 1865 order', p('Juneteenth commemorates the announcement of freedom in Texas associated with General Order No. 3. Major General Gordon Granger issued it in Galveston on June 19, 1865, more than two years after the Emancipation Proclamation. The National Archives preserves the handwritten order in an army record volume. The document announced freedom while also instructing formerly enslaved people about labor and movement. Reading the whole order therefore reveals both emancipation and the paternalistic military framework within which authorities expected freedpeople to begin their lives after slavery.', 'juneteenth')),
    s('community-memory-before-federal-law', 'A commemoration sustained by communities', p('African American communities in Texas began formal Juneteenth celebrations in 1866, and observances later spread to other states. The tradition therefore preceded federal recognition by many generations. That chronology is essential: the 2021 law did not invent the commemoration or create the historical event it remembers. Community practice carried the anniversary through the years between the Civil War and national designation. The Archives account locates the observance in that longer history while connecting the familiar holiday name to the surviving military record of the announcement.', 'juneteenth')),
    s('the-legislative-sequence', 'The federal bill moves through Congress', p('The legislative history printed with Public Law 117–17 records the Senate’s passage on June 15, 2021 and the House’s passage on June 16. Approval followed on June 17. These three dates describe the lawmaking process and should not be confused with the June 19 anniversary. The brief record makes the sequence unusually easy to inspect: passage in each chamber preceded the presidential approval date. National recognition was an act of legislation with a documented chronology, even though the commemoration it recognized had a much longer life.', 'juneteenth-law')),
    s('what-the-act-did', 'A short amendment with a specific purpose', p('The Juneteenth National Independence Day Act amended the federal public-holiday list in Title 5 of the United States Code by adding June 19. Its short text establishes the holiday’s formal name and calendar date. It is a record of federal recognition, not a new emancipation decree or a comprehensive history of slavery’s ending. Reading the law alongside General Order No. 3 keeps those functions separate: the 1865 document concerns freedom in Texas, while the 2021 document gives a commemorative date a place in the federal calendar.', 'juneteenth-law', 'juneteenth')),
  ], [t('June 19, 1865', 'General Order No. 3 announces freedom in Texas.', 'juneteenth'), t('June 15–16, 2021', 'The Senate and House pass the holiday legislation.', 'juneteenth-law'), t('June 17, 2021', 'The act is approved, adding June 19 to federal public holidays.', 'juneteenth-law')], p('The one-page public law establishes the legal change and legislative dates. The National Archives account supplies the 1865 order and community-observance context. The two records concern different actions separated by 156 years; federal recognition should not be mistaken for the beginning of Juneteenth memory.', 'juneteenth-law', 'juneteenth')),

  'ketanji-brown-jackson': essay([
    s('professional-preparation', 'Experience across the federal legal system', p('Ketanji Brown Jackson brought experience in several parts of the legal system to the Supreme Court. After Harvard and Harvard Law School, she held federal clerkships, including one with Justice Stephen Breyer. She worked in private practice, at the U.S. Sentencing Commission, and as an assistant federal public defender in Washington. Later she served on the federal district court in the District of Columbia and the D.C. Circuit Court of Appeals. This professional history places her appointment within a career involving both representation of clients and judicial decision-making.', 'jackson-biography')),
    s('nomination-and-confirmation', 'The process following Breyer’s retirement announcement', p('Breyer announced his intention to retire in January 2022, with the retirement to occur after the Court’s term if a successor had been confirmed. President Joe Biden announced Jackson as his nominee on February 25. Judiciary Committee hearings took place in March, and the Senate confirmed her on April 7 by a vote of 53 to 47. The Library of Congress identifies her as the first Black woman nominated and confirmed to the Court. Confirmation settled the Senate’s decision before the vacancy took effect.', 'jackson-nomination')),
    s('the-june-oaths', 'The beginning of service', p('Jackson took the oaths of office on June 30, 2022, becoming the Court’s 104th associate justice and the first Black woman to serve on it. That date marks the beginning of her Supreme Court service. It followed confirmation by nearly three months and preceded the public investiture in the fall. Maintaining the separate dates clarifies what each milestone accomplished: nomination presented a candidate, confirmation supplied Senate consent, and the oaths accompanied entry into office. An appointment history becomes misleading when those actions are compressed into one ceremonial moment.', 'jackson-oath', 'jackson-nomination')),
    s('investiture-and-historical-first', 'A later ceremonial welcome', p('The Court held a special sitting for Jackson’s formal investiture on September 30, 2022. President Biden and Vice President Kamala Harris attended with their spouses. The Court’s record explicitly distinguishes this ceremony from the June oaths, making it a useful source for interpreting photographs and commemorative accounts of her arrival. The historic first concerns access to membership on the Court. Her individual opinions and influence require study of the judicial record after appointment; they cannot be inferred simply from her identity or the ceremony welcoming her.', 'jackson-oath')),
  ], [t('February 25, 2022', 'Biden announces Jackson as his Supreme Court nominee.', 'jackson-nomination'), t('April 7, 2022', 'The Senate confirms the nomination, 53–47.', 'jackson-nomination'), t('June 30, 2022', 'Jackson takes the oaths of office.', 'jackson-oath'), t('September 30, 2022', 'The Court holds her formal investiture.', 'jackson-oath')], p('The Court’s biography records professional positions; the Library of Congress guide documents nomination and confirmation. The Court’s ceremony page expressly separates the oath date from investiture. These sources establish the appointment milestone without predicting Jackson’s decisions or treating a later ceremony as her first day in office.', 'jackson-biography', 'jackson-nomination', 'jackson-oath')),
};
